                "reviews": [
                    {
                        "recommendationid": "234337963",
                        "author": {
                            "steamid": "76561198006607004",
                            "num_reviews": 10,
                            "playtime_forever": 532,
                            "playtime_last_two_weeks": 0,
                            "playtime_at_review": 389,
                            "last_played": 1788597092
                        },
                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Although obviously an older game, it's still the best \"modern\" subsim there is. Yes, better than [b]Modern Naval Warfare[/b] (2026 early access). If you're looking for an overall modern *strategy* game, [b]Sea Power[/b] is king.\n\nThe only problem is that installing (and configuring it to run great) can be a pain, but if you're a \"navy nut\" like me it's worth doing it. Tip: once you have it running nice, make sure to WinRar your game folder to a backup location for easy quick clean re-install, should you ever need it. You should do this anyway before you begin installing any mods (e.g. Reinforce Alert) so you have a clean \"stock\" fixed install to go back to.\n\nI have spent thousands of hours playing this. The funny thing is that my Steam playtime only lists a few hours; that's 'cos once you have it installed and configured (and added Reinforce Alert), you don't normally run it though Steam again. For me, I either run the exe directly or through DxWnd.\n\nI'm not going to write a full \"for dummies\" tutorial how to set it up since that's already been done over and over ad infinitum but let me just say [b]the key[/b] is to simply install the DgVoodoo2 wrapper (all you need are the d3d8.dll and dgVoodooCpl.exe files) and that's it. [b]Don't forget to right-click on the orange DgVoodoo logo in the control panel to enable its extended menu (for the borderless config)[/b].\n\nIf you want to run it in a window (in whatever size you like) this can be done properly via DxWnd [b](specifically, version 2.04.36)[/b]. I needed to configure the dgvoodoo wrapper first or DxWnd wouldn't work. I found the \"sweet spot\" screen size for me is 1680x1050 since then it's not too stretched and squashed. I don't like its supposedly default 4:3 aspect ratio since for whatever reason it looks strangely \"squashed\" in 4:3. Perhaps (at the time) the devs had intended it to be played on 16:10 ratio monitors, I'm not sure. Whatever, it's all good since 1680x1050 fixes it on a standard 16:9 screen (set 1280x1024 Mode 3 in game options or the font text will be too small and distorted if set higher).\n\nGood hunting and carry on!",
